Precise control of the nature of terminal groups in polymer chains is one of the most important aspects of modern polymer science. Despite being overwhelmed in number by the main-chain monomeric units, the termini have significant influence on the polymer chemical reactivity. End-functionalized polymers are indispensable raw materials used for surfactants,1 block copolymers,2 supramolecular polymers,3 and polymer networks,4 as well as for bio-conjugation compounds.5 Despite their high economic importance, efficient preparation of end-functionalized polymers remains challenging, mainly because of the inefficiency of practical separation techniques with high terminus recognition capability. A key technology for efficient separation of polymers with different functional termini has been in demand from polymer industries as well as modern chemical research in non-specialist laboratories.
Metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) are designable porous materials formed via self-assembly of organic ligands and metal ions.6 They possess high porosities with Angstrom to nanometers sized channels that can accommodate gas molecules as well as more complex guests.7 Thus, the use of MOFs as versatile stationary phases for column chromatographic separation of molecules has been reported in the literature.8–12 Although such separation techniques benefit greatly from the designability of MOFs, they are mainly driven by traditional separation mechanisms, such as affinity partition or size-exclusion, limiting their scope to relatively small molecules, such as hydrocarbons,10 aromatics,11 and fullerenes.12
Recognition and separation of polymers based on small structural differences remain extremely difficult. In particular, polymer termini hardly influence the molecular weight, polarity, solubility, and conformational volume of the parent polymer due to their minor constitutive presence. This makes the separation of polymers differing only in their termini extremely difficult using traditional methods like reprecipitation or size-exclusion chromatography. We recently demonstrated that MOF nano-windows could be used as precisely defined sieves to separate polymers with small or bulky termini, which were inserted or rejected, respectively, affording preparative purifications (Figure 1).13 It has been previously demonstrated that many polymers can be inserted by threading through the MOF nano-windows owing to favorable interactions with the MOF pore walls that compensate the entropic cost of uncoiling.14–16 Therefore, we have had a strong prospect of more precise recognition system by solution phase liquid chromatography using a MOF stationary phase.
Herein we show that MOFs can precisely recognize polymer terminal structures, enabling the first reported chromatographic separation of polymers. To gain preliminary insights into the range of terminus recognition capability of MOFs, we first studied the size correlation between the size of channel aperture of MOFs and termini of the polymer to be accommodated. In this study, polyethylene glycol (PEG) with molecular weight of $2000\mathrm{Da}$ (degree of polymerization, DP, $n=45$ ) was selected and used as the parent polymer (Figure 2).
The insertion capability in MOF nanochannels was evaluated for homo-bifunctional PEGs with hydroxyl (PEG-H), methyl ether (PEG-Me), methacrylate (PEG-MA), benzyl (PEG-Bn), 1-naphthyl (PEG-1Nph), 2-naphthyl (PEG$2\mathbf{Nph})$ , 1-pyrenyl $\left(\mathbf{PEG-Py}\right)$ , and trityl (PEG-Tr) moieties. The MOF $[\mathrm{Zn}_{2}$ (1,4-naphthalenedicarboxylate)2triethylenediamine]n (1; channel aperture s $\mathrm{ize}=5.7\times5.7\mathring{\mathrm{A}}^{2}.$ ),17 with regular one-dimensional channels along the $c$ -axis, was employed as a rigid host (Figure 1). The insertion of PEG into MOFs was first evaluated in the presence of a solvent, according to literature.13,18 A designated amount of 1 and the corresponding mass of PEG for $5\mathrm{wt\%}$ loading were dissolved in acetonitrile. The solvent was then removed under reduced pressure at room temperature, to homogeneously coat the MOF crystals with PEG. The samples were further annealed at $70^{\circ}\mathrm{C},$ above the melting temperature of PEG, to allow further insertion, producing the 1⊃PEG composites.13
The formation of 1⊃PEG composite was evidenced by differential scanning calorimetry (DSC) analysis (Figure 3). Polymers confined in the nano-pores show remarkable shift of their melting peaks in their DSC profiles because of different assembly states from that in the bulk phase.14,18, 19 Indeed, the 1⊃PEG-H composite did not show any peak around the melting point of pristine PEG-H $(49^{\circ}\mathrm{C})$ , indicating complete insertion of PEG-H into 1 (Figure 3). Powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD) analyses suggested no structural degradation of the MOF host after the composite formation (Figure S1). Similarly, DSC analyses indicated complete insertions for PEGMe, PEG-MA, PEG-Bn, PEG-2Nph, and PEG-1Nph, all of which have rather small terminal groups, with approximate projection diameters below ${\sim}0.6\mathrm{nm}$ . In contrast, the composites 1⊃PEG-Py and 1⊃PEG-Tr, which have larger PEG termini, showed clear exothermic melting peaks at 34 and $32^{\circ}\mathrm{C},$ respectively (Figure 3b). This indicates that PEG-Py and PEG-Tr were still present outside of 1, as the bulkier termini prevented the threading of the entire polymer chains into the MOF channels.13 These results are consistent with the channel aperture size of 1 that precisely sieves out polymers with termini larger than ${\sim}0.6\mathrm{nm}$ .
Interestingly, the insertion kinetics of PEGs were dependent on their termini sizes. The kinetics of PEG insertion in 1 was estimated by mixing MOF and the neat polymer in a DSC pan, subjecting it to repeated cycles of heating and cooling, measuring the progressive decrease in melting enthalpy for free PEG outside 1 after each heating cycle, and deducing the inserted quantity from the differences (Figures 4 and S2).13 This analysis showed that PEGs with large termini, viz. PEGPy and PEG-Tr, showed no insertion at all. Meanwhile, other PEGs showed insertion with different kinetic aspects depending on their terminal groups.
Among the PEGs inserted in the MOF, PEG-H, PEG-Me, and PEG-MA showed the fastest insertions, while PEG2Nph insertion was slower and PEG-1Nph insertion was the slowest. The insertion of PEGs could be explained by two kinetic modes, one fast and one slow. The fast process took place within $10\mathrm{min}.$ , during the first heating cycle, and was strongly affected by the terminal groups, as manifested by the vastly different inserted amounts for short heating times. On the other hand, the slow process seemed to affect all the insertable PEGs similarly, because similar kinetic slopes were observed.
The intriguing differences in the fast process are presumably due to the steric hindrance of the terminal groups caused by friction with the channel wall upon insertion, which may appear when the terminal projection size becomes close to the aperture size of the host MOF. The fast process was predominant during the initial stages of the insertion event at the PEG/MOF interface. On the other hand, the slow process could be related to the dynamics and mobility of the PEG chains in the nanochannel, which eventually becomes the rate limiting step in the entire insertion event.
Among these results, the significant difference in insertion kinetics between PEG-1Nph and PEG-2Nph particularly underpins the high potential for discrimination of such minute differences in projection size caused by positional isomerism in terminal groups. Prompted by this terminus-recognition capability, we examined the column chromatographic separation for various PEGs using 1 as the stationary phase. We anticipated that terminus recognition could be amplified by dynamic processes, such as insertion complexation of polymers into MOFs at the interface between the mobile and stationary phases. A packed column (stainless steel, dimensions: $4\mathrm{mm}\phi\times150\mathrm{mm}$ length) was prepared using dry powdery 1, (Particles size: $3\mathrm{-}10~\{\mathrm{um},$ , Figure S3) using a tapping method (Figures 5a and 5b). The 1-packed column was then connected to a high-performance liquid chromatography (HPLC) system using $N,N.$ -dimethylformamide (DMF) as the mobile phase.
