Figure 5c shows the chromatograms obtained for the PEGs at $80~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}.$ Significant retentions were observed for PEG-H, PEG-Me, PEG-MA, PEG-Bn, and PEG-2Nph. In stark contrast, PEG-1Nph, PEG-Py, and PEG-Tr showed no retention by the 1-packed column, thus eluting immediately after injection. The elution times of PEG-Py and PEG-Tr were slightly shorter than the solvent elution time, $t_{0}=1.38\mathrm{min}$ , probably due to the minor effect of the size-exclusion mode. The chromatograms are in good agreement with the trend observed for the neat-phase insertion experiments (Figure 4). PEGs with smaller termini (PEG-H, PEG-Me, PEG-MA, PEG-Bn, and PEG-2Nph) can enter the MOF channel and most likely undergo exchange between the mobile and stationary MOF phases via competing insertion/displacement processes, resulting in their retention in the column. Most strikingly, PEG1Nph showed no significant retention and was eluted immediately at $t_{0},$ whereas PEG-2Nph showed significant retention in the column, resulting in a broad peak at $3.18~\mathrm{min}$ (Figure 5c). This enabled chromatographic separation of the mixture of both PEGs presenting distinguishable elution peaks (Figure S5). We envisage a probable mechanism, in which PEG-2Nph with a straight terminal configuration along the main chain could penetrate smoothly into the MOF channel to be retained, whereas PEG-1Nph could not because of considerable steric hindrance from the sideways oriented termini, thus preventing its insertion. Such small steric difference could be amplified through the dynamic insertion process on the MOF stationary phase, leading to significant chromatographic differences.
In addition to steric effects, differences in chemical natures also influence the retention times of PEGs. Indeed, every insertable PEG showed a unique retention time, depending on the terminal structures. This indicates that in addition to physical sieving due to the termini, complex chemical interactions between the termini, eluting solvent, and the MOF pores contributed to the retention of PEGs. Therefore, MOFs with the highest polymer recognition capabilities can be used as new stationary phases for liquid chromatography that can emerge as a powerful tool for the separation of diverse telechelic polymers. In contrast, conventional size-exclusion chromatography using polystyrene-gel columns (Shodex KD-806M) was ineffective for the separation of these PEGs, showing no appreciable difference in retention times (Figure S6).
The retention of PEGs by the MOF was also largely affected by the column temperature. With decreasing temperature, the retention peaks of PEG-H, PEG-Me, PEG-MA, PEG-Bn, and PEG-2Nph became broader, disappearing completely at $40~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ (Figure S7). This temperature effect can be explained by the adsorption affinity between MOF and PEG. The adsorption of PEG into 1 becomes stronger with lowering temperature because of its exothermic nature.13 While the PEGs with smaller termini were inserted and retained permanently in the column bed at temperatures below $40^{\circ}\mathrm{C},$ , the ones with the larger terminals could still elute immediately, irrespective of the column temperature. In addition, the eluent affected the affinity between PEG and 1. This enabled us to separate a hetero-end functionalized PEG that generally requires the highest difficulty on the current separation/purification techniques. Similarly to homo-bifunctional PEG- $\mathbf{\nabla}\cdot\mathbf{P}\mathbf{y}$ (Figure 5c), a pyrene/methyl hetero-bifunctionalized PEG, which has pyrene and methyl moiety at either end, was eluted immediately from the column when using DMF as the eluent (Figure S8). However, when using ethanol as the eluent, the hetero-bifunctionalized PEG was retained permanently in the column unlike PEG-Py that was still eluted quickly. Such temperature and eluent effects should enable preparative separation, which will be pursued in our future work.
In summary, herein we report the outstanding capability of MOFs to recognize polymer termini, and its use for the column chromatographic separation of end-functionalized PEGs. The purification step in the synthesis of such end-functionalized polymers is generally arduous, leading to high cost and workload. The insertion mechanism and the molecular origin of such high affinity between PEG and 1 will be discussed in successive papers. We envision that polymer terminus recognition by MOFs should pave the way for widespread use in current polymer chemistries and industries, and will greatly facilitate development of a wide range of new materials.
